      en: "Likoma Cathedral, on Likoma Island in Lake Malawi, is one of the largest churches in sub-Saharan Africa. Built between 1903 and 1911 by the Universities' Mission to Central Africa (UMCA), it features Neo-Gothic architecture with a cruciform plan. The cathedral measures 100 meters in length, 50 meters in width, and seats 2,000 people. Its walls are built from local stone and brick, and the interior boasts colorful stained glass windows and a high altar carved from indigenous wood. It serves as the seat of the Anglican Diocese of Northern Malawi. The cathedral is a significant heritage site and a testament to missionary influence in the region.",
